# Cloudflare Pages routing.
#
# The last line is the SPA fallback: a deep link like /pair or /remote is not a
# file, so it has to be served index.html and let the router take it from there.
#
# The first line is what stops that fallback answering for the backend. Without
# it, /api/ping returns index.html with a 200 -- and the interface's own
# reachability check read that as "the API is up", rendered the whole workbench,
# and then got this page back from every call it made. A 404 here is the truth:
# there is no API on a static host, and the interface is built to handle being
# told so.
#
# The destination is `api-404.html` and NOT `404.html`, which is the detail that
# made all of this inert. Pages treats a file named exactly `404.html` as its
# own not-found handler and serves it for every path that is not a static asset
# -- ahead of the catch-all below. So with one in the output, `/pair` and
# `/chat` both returned the literal string "404" and the deployed app was
# reachable only at `/`. A phone opening a pairing link got a 404 page, which is
# what made the QR code look broken after it had started carrying a real URL.
/api/*  /api-404.html  404
/*      /index.html  200
